<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory of Nazianzus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 329–390</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Gregory the Theologian, Gregory Nazianzen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory of Nazianzus, also known as Gregory the Theologian, was an early Christian theologian and prelate who served as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from 380 to 381. Born near Nazianzus in Cappadocia, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> with his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea">Basil of Caesarea</a>, and with Basil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa">Gregory of Nyssa</a> he is counted among the Cappadocian Fathers. He shaped Trinitarian theology and is widely considered the most accomplished rhetorical stylist of the patristic age. Selected to lead the First Council of Constantinople in 381, he resigned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> disputed his position, and spent his last years at Arianzus writing poetry until his death in 390.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nazianzus.jpg" alt="Gregory of Nazianzus" width="300" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_of_Nazianzus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gregory was born on the family estate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> near the village of Arianzus, outside Nazianzus in south-west Cappadocia, to wealthy landowners. His father, also called Gregory, had been a Hypsistarian until his wife Nonna converted him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 325, and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Nazianzus in 328 or 329. Gregory studied rhetoric and philosophy at Nazianzus, Caesarea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he became close friends with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea">Basil of Caesarea</a> and met the future emperor Julian. On the voyage to Athens a violent storm led him to vow to dedicate his life to Christ.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 361 his father had him ordained a presbyter, which Gregory, who had thought of a monastic life, called an act of tyranny. After Emperor Julian declared himself against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> he composed his Invectives Against Julian in 362 and 363. In 372 Basil ordained him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Sasima, a post he disliked and called a horse-stop on the main road, and he returned to assist his dying father at Nazianzus. After his parents died in 374 he gave most of his inheritance to the poor.</p><p>In 379 the synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> asked him to lead the Nicene cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. From a chapel in a cousin's villa, which he named Anastasia, he delivered five discourses on the Trinity. An Arian mob burst into the church on the vigil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> and wounded him. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> arrived in 380 and expelled the Arian bishop Demophilus, Gregory was enthroned at the Basilica of the Apostles. In the spring of 381 he led the First Council of Constantinople after the death of Meletius of Antioch, but Egyptian and Macedonian bishops contested his position as canonically illegitimate. He resigned, and Theodosius accepted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gregory returned to Cappadocia and by 383 withdrew to Arianzus, where he wrote much of his poetry, including an autobiographical poem of nearly 2,000 lines. He died about 25 January 390. He is noted for his work on the Holy Spirit, being the first to use procession to describe its relation to the Godhead. The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> cited his orations as authoritative in 431, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451 named him Theologus, a title held by only two others. His relics were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 950, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1204 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> on 27 November 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gregory of Nazianzus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For that which He has not assumed He has not healed; but that which is united to His Godhead is also saved.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter 101, to Cledonius &#183; tr. Charles Gordon Browne and James Edward Swallow, 1894 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3103a.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
